\section{The family decides the space, and 4-in-6 is IPv6}{


An IPv4 address is never inside an IPv6 block and an IPv6 address is never
inside an IPv4 one, so those pairs are \code{FALSE} rather than an error -- a
mixed denylist is an ordinary thing to hold. A \code{v6_4in6} address such as
\verb{::ffff:192.0.2.1} searches the \strong{IPv6} space: it is inside
\verb{::ffff:0:0/96} and it is \emph{not} inside \verb{192.0.2.0/24}, because it is a
128-bit address that happens to embed an IPv4 one. That embedding is a
separate fact, reported by \code{\link[=addr_embeddings]{addr_embeddings()}}; testing the address it
contains means naming that address. This is the width rule of
\code{\link[=addr_to_bytes]{addr_to_bytes()}} in its containment form.
}

\section{A block is the question, so a bad block is an error}{


The decoders elsewhere in raddr return a missing value for input they cannot
read. Blocks are the exception, because a block is not data being read -- it
is the question being asked. A denylist entry that silently matched nothing
would be a hole in the denylist that the caller has no way to see. So a
malformed block, a missing one, a prefix length outside \code{0:32} or \code{0:128},
and a block with \strong{host bits set} all error, and the message names the fix.

\verb{192.168.1.1/24} is refused rather than masked to \verb{192.168.1.0/24}, because
it is equally likely to be a host someone meant to write \verb{/32}. A missing
\emph{address} is still a missing answer: \code{NA}, never \code{FALSE}.
}

